    "readout_text": "To determine the value of $ f(2023) $, we are given the functional equation:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{d \\mid n} d \\cdot f\\left(\\frac{n}{d}\\right) = 1 \\quad \\text{for all positive integers } n.\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Reformulating the Equation\n\nWe start by reindexing the sum. Let $ k = \\frac{n}{d} $, so $ d = \\frac{n}{k} $, and the sum becomes:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{k \\mid n} \\frac{n}{k} \\cdot f(k) = 1.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $ n $, we get:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{k \\mid n} \\frac{f(k)}{k} = \\frac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\nDefine a new function $ g(n) = \\frac{f(n)}{n} $. Then the above becomes:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{k \\mid n} g(k) = \\frac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\nThis suggests a connection to Möbius inversion. If we define $ h(n) = \\frac{1}{n} $, then the equation is:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{k \\mid n} g(k) = h(n).\n$$\n\nBy Möbius inversion, we can recover $ g(n) $ as:\n\n$$\ng(n) = \\sum_{d \\mid n} \\mu(d) \\cdot h\\left(\\frac{n}{d}\\right).\n$$\n\nSubstituting back $ h(n) = \\frac{1}{n} $, we get:\n\n$$\ng(n) = \\sum_{d \\mid n} \\mu(d) \\cdot \\frac{d}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $ g(n) = \\frac{f(n)}{n} $, we find:\n\n$$\nf(n) = \\sum_{d \\mid n} \\mu(d) \\cdot d.\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Applying the Formula\n\nNow we compute $ f(2023) $ using the formula:\n\n$$\nf(2023) = \\sum_{d \\mid 2023} \\mu(d) \\cdot d.\n$$\n\nFirst, factorize $ 2023 $:\n\n$$\n2023 = 7 \\cdot 17^2.\n$$\n\nThe divisors of 2023 are:\n\n$$\n1, 7, 17, 119, 289, 2023.\n$$\n\nNow compute $ \\mu(d) \\cdot d $ for each:\n\n- $ \\mu(1) = 1 \\Rightarrow 1 \\cdot 1 = 1 $\n- $ \\mu(7) = -1 \\Rightarrow -1 \\cdot 7 = -7 $\n- $ \\mu(17) = -1 \\Rightarrow -1 \\cdot 17 = -17 $\n- $ \\mu(119) = \\mu(7 \\cdot 17) = (-1)^2 = 1 \\Rightarrow 1 \\cdot 119 = 119 $\n- $ \\mu(289) = \\mu(17^2) = 0 \\Rightarrow 0 \\cdot 289 = 0 $\n- $ \\mu(2023) = \\mu(7 \\cdot 17^2) = 0 \\Rightarrow 0 \\cdot 2023 = 0 $\n\nSumming these:\n\n$$\nf(2023) = 1 - 7 - 17 + 119 + 0 + 0 = 96.\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Verifying via Multiplicativity\n\nThe function $ f(n) = \\sum_{d \\mid n} \\mu(d) \\cdot d $ is multiplicative since both $ \\mu(d) $ and $ d $ are multiplicative functions. Thus, for $ n = 7 \\cdot 17^2 $, we can compute:\n\n- $ f(7) = \\mu(1) \\cdot 1 + \\mu(7) \\cdot 7 = 1 - 7 = -6 $\n- $ f(17^2) = \\mu(1) \\cdot 1 + \\mu(17) \\cdot 17 + \\mu(17^2) \\cdot 289 = 1 - 17 + 0 = -16 $\n\nThen:\n\n$$\nf(2023) = f(7) \\cdot f(17^2) = (-6) \\cdot (-16) = 96.\n$$\n\nThis confirms the result.\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer\n\n$$\n\\boxed{96}\n$$\n\n\\confidence{Almost certain}",
